            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous retroperitoneal hemorrhage in a dialysis patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e217-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jemermed.2012.02.048</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0736-4679(12)00340-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) often present to the Emergency Department with a multitude of complaints. Abnormal vital signs and a chief complaint of pain can produce a diagnosis ranging from a cardiac etiology to an infectious source.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Our goal is to discuss a case in which an ESRD dialysis patient presented with a rare, life-threatening emergency.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 55-year-old man with ESRD presented with acute abdominal pain and hypotension caused by a spontaneous retroperitoneal hemorrhage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This case report demonstrates the necessity of having broad differential diagnoses when evaluating patients with ESRD.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
